        <h4 class="font-bold text-ink text-base">1. Electrostatic Taylor Cone Formation &amp; Critical Field Threshold</h4>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed">
          The balance between normal electrostatic Maxwell stress and capillary surface tension determines the critical onset electric field (<span class="font-mono">E_crit</span>) required to establish a steady conical meniscus:
        </p>
        <div class="p-3 bg-surface-soft rounded-xl font-mono text-xs text-emerald-400 overflow-x-auto">
          E_crit = &radic;[ (2 &bull; &gamma;) / (&epsilon;_0 &bull; r_capillary) ]; &nbsp; &theta;_Taylor = 49.3&deg;; &nbsp; d_droplet &prop; [ (&rho; &bull; &epsilon;_0 &bull; Q&sup2;) / (&gamma; &bull; I&sup2;) ]^(1/6)
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed">
          Operating at <span class="font-mono">E / E_crit = 1.02 – 1.08</span> establishes the stable cone-jet regime, emitting a continuous sub-micron liquid filament that breaks up into monodisperse charged droplets below 1.5 µm without spray pulsing.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Principle 2 -->
