        <h4 class="font-bold text-ink text-base">2. Supercritical Antisolvent Phase Equilibrium &amp; Nucleation Rate</h4>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed">
          Supercritical CO2 diffuses rapidly into organic droplets while solvent expands into the dense fluid phase, triggering instantaneous solute supersaturation (<span class="font-mono">S</span>):
        </p>
        <div class="p-3 bg-surface-soft rounded-xl font-mono text-xs text-cyan-400 overflow-x-auto">
          S = C(t) / C_eq(P, T, x_CO2); &nbsp; J_nucleation = A &bull; exp[ - (16 &bull; &pi; &bull; &gamma;_SL&sup3; &bull; &nu;&sup2;) / (3 &bull; k_B&sup3; &bull; T&sup3; &bull; (ln S)&sup2;) ]
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed">
          At <span class="font-mono">S &gt; 120</span>, the primary nucleation rate <span class="font-mono">J</span> increases by 10¹⁴ orders of magnitude, causing explosive burst nucleation where millions of sub-micron nuclei consume solute before crystal growth can create macro-aggregates.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Principle 3 -->
      <div class="bg-surface p-5 rounded-2xl border border-hairline space-y-3">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-ink text-base">3. Binary Mass Transfer &amp; Microsecond Solvent Stripping Kinetics</h4>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed">
          The characteristic solvent drying time (<span class="font-mono">&tau;_evap</span>) scales quadratically with droplet diameter, reducing from seconds to microseconds due to electrostatic nano-droplet generation:
        </p>
        <div class="p-3 bg-surface-soft rounded-xl font-mono text-xs text-emerald-400 overflow-x-auto">
          &tau;_evap = d_droplet&sup2; / [ 8 &bull; D_CO2-solvent &bull; ln(1 + B_M) ]; &nbsp; D_12 &sim; (k_B &bull; T) / (6 &bull; &pi; &bull; &mu;_sc &bull; r_solvent)
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed">
          Because droplet diameter is atomized from &gt;30 µm down to &lt;1.5 µm, drying duration collapses from &gt;2500 ms to &lt;15 ms, stripping ethanol/ethyl acetate down to &lt;1.0 ppm residual solvent under USP &lt;467&gt;.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Principle 4 -->
      <div class="bg-surface p-5 rounded-2xl border border-hairline space-y-3">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-ink text-base">4. Noyes-Whitney Dissolution Velocity &amp; Bioavailability Gain</h4>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed">
          Dissolution velocity in gastrointestinal fluid increases inversely with particle diameter according to the classical Noyes-Whitney equation:
        </p>
        <div class="p-3 bg-surface-soft rounded-xl font-mono text-xs text-amber-400 overflow-x-auto">
          dM/dt = [ D_aq &bull; A_surface &bull; (C_s - C_bulk) ] / h_diff; &nbsp; A_surface / V = 6 / d_50 (120&times; Specific Surface Area)
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed">
          Reducing poorly soluble bioactive crystals from 25 µm to 145 nm increases effective surface area by over 120-fold, driving 15-minute in-vitro dissolution from &lt;1.5% to &gt;99.1% without surfactant toxicity.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

